Old version build for ml505 XUP board, using Xilinx ISE software.
Old version had an abundance of customizations & fluf which I
  attempted to remove where easy to do so, going for minimal
  working modern version.

New version modernizes to use Pynq-Z2 and/or Arty A7 boards,
  which implies porting to Vivado at the same time. Major board
  differences are access to DRAM on the Pynq (indirect via AXI
  for shared DMA, connected through the PS (ARM cpu of the Zynq).
  The Arty A7 has DRAM/DDR straight to logic (PL), as there is
  no Zynq CPU! However, the Pynq boards have DVI/HDMI output
  port, and the Arty A7 needs addition of a PMOD (to VGA)!

Berkeley cs151 skeletons (2019 & 2024) were used as the basis
  of a modern-style "Makefile & Vivado TCL scripts" based build
  flow. "IP" is utilized differently, such as capturing then
  customizing the output of a clock-wizard. Use of a MIG for
  DDR/DRAM access presents some challenges too (using TCL to
  rebuild the "IP" or capturing the output of the IP.

Before branch/forking, the "software" content was separately
  updated to build successfully using an APT/deb install of
  a GCC based MIPS cross compiler. The "/bin/<scripts>" were
  also updated to Python 3.
